  <title xml:lang="EN">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Indian House Crow Eradication Programme</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2" xml:lang="EN">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Indian House Crow (Corvus Splendens) is an alien invasive species native of the Indian Sub-Continent. It was introduced in Zanzibar around 1890 mainly to feed on garbage in rubbish dumps as one of the strategies to clean towns. Numbers remained low for many years but the crows later spread to the mainland and the first sighting of two crows was made in 1951 in Honeymoon Island off Dar es Salaam. The crows continued to multiply at alarming rates and in 2006/07 the bird population was estimated to be over eight hundred thousand mainly in Dar ES Salaam, but slowly expanding to nearby places like Kibaha, Mlandizi, Kibaha, Morogoro and Tanga.</narrative>
  </description>
